## Write

Answer with one JSON object in the shape below and nothing else.

Voice: plain, short, friendly. Sentence case. No hype. Write for a person who reads the text in half a minute. Keep each sentence under 20 words with one fact in it. Cut filler such as "it looks like", "you may want to", "this is a" or "worth a look", and do not explain what a product or tool does. Use the words the person sees in PostHog. Never an em dash or an en dash; use a comma or a new sentence. Never quote customer text, never name customers or people, and never name a time of day: the page greets the person itself and the text stays up for hours.

- `headline`: one sentence that counts the items and says what they need, true for every item. For example "Three reports need your input" only when all three wait for the person, else "Five reports need your attention".
- `paragraphs`: two to four short paragraphs, at most {{ max_words }} words in total. Each paragraph is a list of segments `{"text", "item_key"}`, and the page shows each paragraph as its own block.
  - The first paragraph is about the top item only, in one or two sentences: what it is, and why it comes first.
  - The next paragraphs group the other items by what the person does with them, for example the reports that wait for a decision, the reports to review, and the reports nobody owns. Keep the item order inside a group.
  - A paragraph has at most three sentences. Start a new paragraph rather than add a fourth.
  - For each item, say what the person can do, not only what is wrong: decide, review, merge a fix that is ready, or pick up an unowned report. Use the item's `reason`, `status` and `implementation_pr` for this.
  - Vary the sentences. Do not start every sentence the same way.
  - Never list items ("Also ready: X, Y and Z") and never start a sentence with "Also".
- A linked segment names exactly one item through its `item_key`: a natural noun phrase of at most {{ max_link_words }} words that starts with a word. Link every item exactly once. Put the link inside a sentence, after a word such as "The", so that every sentence starts with a capital letter. Plain text segments have `item_key` null and carry their own spaces.
- `items`: one entry for each item in <items>, in the same order:
  - `key`: the item's key, exactly as given.
  - `label`: at most {{ max_label_words }} words that say what it is.
  - `signal`: the short fact that makes it matter, at most {{ max_signal_chars }} characters. Examples: "Waits for your input", "P1, fix ready", "P0, nobody owns it".

This example shows the shape only. Square brackets mark the linked segments. Do not copy its words or facts:

> The [signup form report] comes first. It waits for your decision, and a fix is ready to review.
>
> Two reports wait for your review. A fix for the [billing page crash] is ready to merge. The [slow funnel query] needs a check of its numbers.
>
> Nobody owns the [export failure] yet. It is a P0, so pick it up or assign it.

```json
{
  "headline": "string",
  "paragraphs": [[{"text": "string", "item_key": "string or null"}]],
  "items": [{"key": "string", "label": "string", "signal": "string"}]
}
```
